00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:49.307
So starting off with a brief
history, this is the CR48.

00:01:49.307 --> 00:01:51.140
This was actually the
very first Chromebook.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.940
That was back in 2010.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.450
And Chrome browser has
been picking up all

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.450
across the world, right?

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.450
A lot of workloads were
moving to the web and people

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.640
within Google thought,
what if we could actually

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.940
make a computer that is built
for this world where everything

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.730
is in the Cloud?

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.560
What if your
computer didn't suck?

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.250
It didn't take
minutes to boot up.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.660
It could boot up in seconds.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.490
What if all your apps
and all your data

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:16.670
were actually delivered from the
Cloud, and stored in the Cloud,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.710
so that you can
secure them remotely

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:20.510
and you can deliver
them remotely?

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:22.640
And what happened was,
a bunch of engineers

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.730
actually got together and
hacked what you see here.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.950
It was a Chrome
browser in full screen.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.580
They removed a lot of the bloat
in existing operating systems,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.000
things that we check, for
example, for the floppy disk,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.290
so move that away.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.330
And then have a very,
very slimmed down run

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:40.282
time, which ran only
the Chrome browser.

00:02:40.282 --> 00:02:41.990
And what they had was
this Chrome browser

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.030
running in full
screen and a keyboard,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.450
and that was basically a book
with Chrome and Chromebook.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.160
So this was the Genesis.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.531
And since then, a lot of meat
has been added to the OS,

00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:53.030
right?

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:55.670
There's been a more elaborate
runtime system for apps,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.410
there's been a launcher shelf--

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:01.160
everything you tend to relate to
a traditional operating system

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:02.090
environment.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.510
But throughout all
this and since then,

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.500
we've actually never let go of
the founding principles which

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.255
were speed,
simplicity, security.

00:03:10.255 --> 00:03:12.380
So even seven days down,
seven years down the road,

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.420
this thing still boots
up in five seconds even

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.454
with the [INAUDIBLE]
system, which

00:03:16.454 --> 00:03:18.620
we're going to talk about
later with the Play Store.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.130
We still boot up
in five seconds.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.400
So this is the joke now,
that Chrome OS is actually

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.044
the fastest booting
Android phone.

00:03:24.044 --> 00:03:24.960
And that is very true.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.660
Five seconds you're in, you can
actually use your Android apps.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.329
So we've never really
sacrificed on this.

00:03:30.329 --> 00:03:32.120
Simplicity is, again,
one of these things--

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.020
both user-facing simplicity
and admin-facing simplicity.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.270
It should be very easy to use.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.490
There's no clutter
on the desktop.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:38.060
Your apps are there.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:38.851
Your data is there.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.070
It's all very clear to you.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.611
And even from an
admin's perspective,

00:03:41.611 --> 00:03:43.820
it's very, very easy to
scale for tens of thousands

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.480
of devices in a fleet with very
little management overhead.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.070
And finally,
security, and this has

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.650
been really the
cornerstone of Chrome OS

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.275
because we've built it--

00:03:53.275 --> 00:03:54.650
always every single
feature we've

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.680
added, from the very basic
boot sequence of the OS,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.230
all the way up to the apps
that are on the system,

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.046
they've been built
with security in mind.

00:04:02.046 --> 00:04:03.920
And we're going to talk
a lot more about that

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.780
during this talk.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.100
So what happened since then?

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.180
Since then, and if we fast
forward now to Q2 2016,

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.285
we actually became
the number one device.

00:04:14.285 --> 00:04:17.000
Back in 2016, we actually
became the number one device

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.170
in education.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.246
And the schools actually
loved that value proposition

00:04:21.246 --> 00:04:23.120
of speed, security,
simplicity, but even went

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.191
beyond that for them.

00:04:24.191 --> 00:04:26.690
Sheer ability was a big thing
because if you look at schools

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.315
and how they deploy Chromebooks,
they just bring them in a cart

00:04:29.315 --> 00:04:30.470
into a class.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.900
And then, students just
pick up a Chromebook,

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.700
any Chromebook, from
the cart, log in,

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.460
and they have everything
they need there.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:37.610
And we're going to
talk more about that.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.109
And Seb is going to talk about
how even enterprise customers

00:04:40.109 --> 00:04:42.590
are using such models in
deployment, what is actually

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.211
a very, very interesting thing.

00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:45.710
Another thing that
they latched onto

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:47.900
was management
and manageability.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.060
Because schools
traditionally have had,

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.250
they're not extremely
tech savvy and they

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.210
have very little IT resources.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.960
So now, I'm a district and
I have 100,000 students.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.780
How do I actually deploy
Chromebooks across the 100,000?

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.240
And today, we have
examples in the field where

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.950
you have tens of
thousands of Chromebooks

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:07.270
with maybe two or three
IT admins supporting them

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:07.820
full-time.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.278
And this is something
that, really,

00:05:09.278 --> 00:05:11.210
you cannot do except
with a Chrome device,

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.640
out of all the options
you have on the market.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.480
And riding on all that momentum,
what happened in Q2 2016

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.460
was, IDC came out with
this report and said,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.340
actually Chromebooks have
been outselling Macs in the US

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.040
for the first time
since their inception.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:28.080
And this was a very
important milestone for us

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.520
because, at this point, we
felt really, really good.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.820
And we knew we were going
in the right direction

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.310
because, not only did we
think this was a good idea,

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.364
but, actually, the market
was voting for this.

00:05:37.364 --> 00:05:39.030
And people were voting
with their money.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.790
And for us, this is really
very, very important signal.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.082
And we don't take this lightly.

00:05:43.082 --> 00:05:44.456
We're actually
very happy with it

00:05:44.456 --> 00:05:46.560
and you just, we're
going to keep innovating.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.232
We're going to keep driving
things in this direction.

00:05:49.232 --> 00:05:51.690
And a lot of the stuff we're
going to tell you about today,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.570
also, in terms of what to look
out for with this product,

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:58.620
we tend to keep this trend
going forward and moving

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.770
and taking more and more
market share in the laptop

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.080
and the convertible segment.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.920
And we didn't stop at EDU.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.650
Because this was
great and it was

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:09.930
great to have this very nice
use case with education,

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.390
but we actually focused
more on enterprise.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:15.060
Because what happened was,
all these value propositions

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.670
actually ring very true for
the enterprise as well--

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.920
speed, simplicity, security
is a big thing, right?

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.990
Shareability, manageability,
they're all things

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.550
that enterprises really want.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.790
And what happened was, even
without any strong push

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.366
from us, small and
medium-sized businesses

00:06:29.366 --> 00:06:30.990
were pulling Chromebooks
off the shelf,

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.830
just like regular
consumers were doing,

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.596
and then were using
them in the workplace.

00:06:34.596 --> 00:06:35.970
And what we wanted
to do, we went

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.280
and talked to these customers
and figured, why do you

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.420
actually like your Chromebook?

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.545
And our intuition was
correct, that they actually--

00:06:41.545 --> 00:06:43.230
all these things
that we care about

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.970
and we're developing
in the product,

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:49.050
they loved and they
were buying into it.

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.090
And we asked them,
what more do you need?

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.920
And we got a big laundry list.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.130
And then, we of course, had
over work cut out for us.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.630
And we've been spending the
last two or three years building

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.070
for the enterprise.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:00.510
And the results
have been amazing

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.217
because, for one, this number
you've seen here on this,

00:07:03.217 --> 00:07:04.800
we're selling almost
a million laptops

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.380
into the market every year now.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.390
And this is a very, very
major milestone for us

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.820
because, again, it proves
that we are on the right trend

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:12.540
there.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.660
And we're going to keep
going in this direction.

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.990
The other one is,
we've also signed up

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.060
a lot of big, big companies.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:22.920
And this has been
our transition,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.070
from just supporting this
very small and medium-sized

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:27.820
businesses were
already on G Suite,

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.390
and going to these
more larger companies.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.180
So we wanted to make sure that,
even in very complex computing

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.587
environments, if you
think of enterprises--

00:07:35.587 --> 00:07:37.920
about their networking needs,
about their virtualization

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.672
needs, about their
security needs--

00:07:41.672 --> 00:07:43.380
it's not easy to come
with an option that

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.010
satisfies all the, that
checks all the boxes

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:48.330
for these enterprises.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.490
So, after a lot of
hard work, and we've

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.710
been able to sign up
a lot of major deals,

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.720
and we've seen
Chromebooks become

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.310
more and more of a
viable alternative

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.380
for these big companies
and in very, very

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:00.597
complex environments.

00:08:00.597 --> 00:08:02.430
And of course, this
is-- we're going to keep

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:03.280
investing in this field.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.460
We're going to make sure that
whenever you take a Chromebook,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.250
even if you have a very complex
set-up in terms of networking,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.610
in terms of identity,
in terms of apps,

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.260
that Chromebooks
can actually compete

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:18.120
with any other device that can
be out there on the market.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.090
So what I want to do now--
and this is, give you

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.762
a bit of the genesis of
this, where it started

00:08:23.762 --> 00:08:25.470
and how we took those
founding principles

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.210
and built this operating
system around it

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.310
and all the major milestones
we've passed through.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:31.435
What I want to do is deep
dive a little bit, right?

00:08:31.435 --> 00:08:34.559
How did we take this device--
that was just the browser

00:08:34.559 --> 00:08:37.350
and was built for the Cloud--
and how do we make it, now,

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.919
a mature operating system that
keeps building for the Cloud?

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:44.460
So we start from a very
basic level, right,

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.070
even on the operating
system level.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:48.570
What you should
know about Chrome OS

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.020
is that, actually,
we provide the images

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.510
and we deliver the images
for the operating systems.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:58.200
And it's very different from
traditional operating systems,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.690
take example, Windows laptops,
you need your IT admins

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.170
to have images that then
they take on a stick

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.640
and then they put and
they burn on the hardware.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.060
That is not true of Chrome OS.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.434
Chrome OS, Google manufactures
all the images, of course,

00:09:09.434 --> 00:09:10.560
in partnership with OEMs.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.960
But then, we deliver
them off of our Cloud.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.840
And this makes a very,
very big difference

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.210
because now we know which
versions of the operating

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.440
systems are out there.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.540
And we can push things
like management, like apps

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.020
onto those operating
systems and we

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:27.420
can make sure that all the
systems are up-to-date.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.330
And this is very, very important
because it has very strong

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.130
security implications.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.530
We know that if any
vulnerability comes in a Chrome

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:37.380
device we can actually patch
it and update all the devices

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.050
in the field within
24 to 48 hours.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:41.640
And this goes back
to the fact that we

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:43.920
know of all these images
and we provided these images

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.280
in the first place.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:49.824
So here, the Cloud perspective,
even on this very low level,

00:09:49.824 --> 00:09:51.240
which is for the
operating system,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:53.448
plays in and allows us to
deliver an experience first

00:09:53.448 --> 00:09:55.380
of all, that is uniform
across all devices

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:58.630
and that is very, very secure.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:03.324
Now, we take this a step further
because the reason admins say,

00:10:03.324 --> 00:10:05.240
for example, have to
burn images onto hardware

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.889
is because they want to provide
a certain experience, right?

00:10:07.889 --> 00:10:10.180
And what you want to do is,
not just deliver the image,

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.957
but deliver all the capabilities
on top of that image.

00:10:12.957 --> 00:10:15.040
So now we have the management
layer for Chrome OS.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:17.290
And this is where you start
customizing the experience

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:19.450
for both your users
and your devices.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:22.047
So we support north of up to
100 policies on the device,

00:10:22.047 --> 00:10:24.130
and these deal with anything
from user management,

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:25.810
device management,
app management,

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:27.380
and network management.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:28.576
So now you get this device--

00:10:28.576 --> 00:10:29.950
true, you can not
burn the image,

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:32.860
but then you can go to a web
console, click a few buttons,

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:35.660
and all those images
just transform

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.650
and they become tailored
to your use cases,

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.210
to your users use cases.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.530
And if we take a look a bit
deeper into each one of these,

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:46.300
for example user
management, you can

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.820
define which URL's
in the browser

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:51.640
the user is actually allowed
to go to and which are not.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:55.339
You can find which printers
a certain user can use.

00:10:55.339 --> 00:10:57.130
And this is very
important because now, you

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:58.720
can actually tailor
the experience

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:00.310
for that particular user.

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:02.449
And these policies,
because they're

00:11:02.449 --> 00:11:04.240
delivered from the
Cloud, they can actually

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:05.682
follow the user
anywhere they go.

00:11:05.682 --> 00:11:07.390
So the user picks up
another device, even

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.130
their personal device,
and they log in,

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.050
these policies follow
the user around.

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:12.640
So now, it's very,
very interesting

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:15.400
because you have the same
management instance regardless

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.380
of which device
the user is using.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.820
And this will come to
mean many things, right?

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:25.770
It means now you can have a cart
model where you just bring cart

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:28.360
in and users can pick up
any device they want, mainly

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:30.490
because, as soon as they
sign in all the policies

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:33.040
that need to apply to them will
apply and the experience they

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.314
need to have will
be on that device.

00:11:35.314 --> 00:11:37.980
If you look at device management
you can set things like sign-in

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:41.260
restrictions, who can and
cannot sign-in into the device.

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:44.210
So that allows you to
actually secure your devices.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.720
You can set things
like [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.000
and even configure these
devices to be public sessions

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:49.702
or kiosks.

00:11:49.702 --> 00:11:51.160
Now, this is very,
very interesting

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.250
because now you can use your
devices in different ways.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.670
Suppose it has been with
user first for one year,

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:58.867
but then you want to
repurpose for something else.

00:11:58.867 --> 00:12:00.700
You actually don't have
to touch the device,

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:01.990
you just send it somewhere.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:04.240
Click a few buttons on the
Cloud and then the device

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.440
is automatically repurposed.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:09.520
So now it was something the
user was using every day

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:13.780
to do their work, but now it's
a kiosk session in your lobby

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:20.650
where users can come in and
do work across the device.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:23.050
Now, we look at app management.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:25.270
And this is also
important because you

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:27.040
don't want to just
manage the devices,

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:31.120
you also want to deliver apps on
which users can do their work.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.040
And this happens also from
the Cloud because you have--

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.170
all apps that can be
delivered to the Chrome device

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:39.878
can only be delivered
from the Play Store

00:12:39.878 --> 00:12:41.146
or from the Chrome Web Store.

00:12:41.146 --> 00:12:42.520
And again, you go
to the console,

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:44.920
you click a few buttons, and the
users have the apps they need.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:46.294
And the same
applies for networks

00:12:46.294 --> 00:12:49.150
as well, VPNs, certificate
provisioning, flows,

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:51.220
all of that can just be
pushed from the Cloud

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:53.560
and tailor made for
the particular users

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:57.530
without ever having to
touch the device itself.

00:12:57.530 --> 00:12:59.800
Now, a little bit of note
on app delivery, and this

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:05.860
is actually quite interesting
because since all the apps that

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:11.120
can come to the device
can come from the Cloud,

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:16.135
there are many things that
become possible, right?

00:13:16.135 --> 00:13:19.700
These app policies are
actually user policies.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.150
So again, a user can just
sign-in into any device

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:24.575
and those apps are delivered
within that user session.

00:13:24.575 --> 00:13:26.200
So that makes it
very, very interesting

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.720
and would not be possible
otherwise with, say,

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:30.970
for example, if you have
executables on a stick, which

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:32.170
are distributing.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:36.340
So there is a lot of benefit
for all these workflows actually

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.620
coming from the Cloud
because you can control them

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.290
easily and remotely and
this allows you to scale,

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:45.250
as you mentioned, for example,
with schools with 100,000 users

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.620
with three or four IT admins.

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:49.990
So it becomes a huge
benefit with scale

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:54.290
and you don't have to maintain
servers to deliver apps,

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.950
you don't have to maintain
servers to deliver files

00:13:56.950 --> 00:14:01.660
and operating system updates.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:04.810
And now, once everything is
in place for your users--

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:06.460
of course your users
on those devices

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.124
are going to be doing all sorts
of stuff and generating data.

00:14:09.124 --> 00:14:10.540
They might be
making modifications

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:13.150
to the operating system,
adding bookmarks, pinning items

00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:14.410
to the shelf.

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:18.160
And we have many technologies,
Chrome sync, Android backup

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:21.280
and restore, Google Drive, all
these technologies actually

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:23.590
allow you to save all
that state that users

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.392
generate in the Cloud.

00:14:25.392 --> 00:14:27.600
And this is very, very
interesting because the moment

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:30.430
that your users go somewhere
else on another device

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:33.370
that state comes
back to them, right?

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:36.070
And so you don't have
to worry that something

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:39.340
got lost because a device
got broken or lost.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:42.100
The user can just move to
another device, any device,

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:46.271
and all that stuff would
actually follow them around.

00:14:46.271 --> 00:14:47.770
And, last but not
least, I just want

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.840
to tie this in with
security because its such

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.960
a core principle
for us, and show you

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:56.290
that for us, security is not
just client-side security.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:58.450
Our goal, of course, is
client-side security,

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:02.560
but we actually leverage
the Cloud a lot with that.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:04.559
One example of that
is delivery of apps.

00:15:04.559 --> 00:15:06.850
If you look at the Play Store
and the Chrome Web Store,

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.980
we actually have automated
systems in the Cloud

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:10.406
to do malware detection.

00:15:10.406 --> 00:15:13.030
This means you don't need to do
malware detection on the client

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:15.100
because the combination
of server malware

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:17.530
detection and
management on the client

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:20.500
allows you to install only
a very specific set of apps

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:21.820
and nothing else.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:25.150
And you offload that task
of checking for malware

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:27.430
and scanning malware
to Google because we

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.580
have very elaborate
systems on our backend.

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:32.440
And the moment
malware is actually

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:36.010
detected we have the ability
to remove all, you, and even

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:38.080
Google, has the ability
to remove that very, very

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:41.110
malicious app if it's
detected from any system

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:42.620
out there in the world.

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:46.810
Another one is, as I
mentioned, OS and app updates,

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:50.387
which happens automatically
from Google servers.

00:15:50.387 --> 00:15:51.970
And this is very
important because you

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.750
don't need an admin in between
to actually take that patch

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:57.179
and apply it to the devices.

00:15:57.179 --> 00:15:59.470
And first of all, it removes
the burden from the admin,

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:01.900
but second of all, it also
removes the margin of error.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:05.110
You can't forget a patch
or you can't have a system

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.335
out there in the field that
is just not being updated.

00:16:08.335 --> 00:16:10.960
The third one is management and,
as you mentioned, because this

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:12.760
is coming from the
Cloud we can actually

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:14.680
make sure that it
follows the user around.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.230
And this is what we mean by
cross-device policy compliance,

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.360
you can make sure that
your users on any device

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:20.294
are compliant.

00:16:20.294 --> 00:16:22.210
And this has very strong
security implications

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:25.780
because if you have certain
policies in place meant

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:29.140
to secure the endpoint, you can
be sure that these policies are

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.042
following the user around.

00:16:31.042 --> 00:16:32.500
And, last but not
least, I actually

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:34.791
want to tell you about one
new technology we developed,

00:16:34.791 --> 00:16:36.310
which is verified access.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.260
And this is, again,
uses a combination

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:40.107
of client and server.

00:16:40.107 --> 00:16:42.190
And this allows you to
cryptographically guarantee

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:44.110
the integrity of your endpoints.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.820
So we know that Chrome OS
always goes in very fine boot

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.940
and you can guarantee
that the code that

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:50.800
is running on the
system has been

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:53.740
verified to be signed by
Google and deployed by Google.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:56.800
So we have a good sense
whether malware actually

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.000
exists on the device.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.730
But then again, on your servers
where you have sensitive data--

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:04.569
so for example,
on the server that

00:17:04.569 --> 00:17:07.180
provides a certificate for the
user to access the network,

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:09.040
or the server that
provides email--

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.640
you can actually
come back and ask,

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:12.730
is this a Chrome device
and verified boot

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.550
and is this a Chrome device that
has enterprise policy applied

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:16.329
to it?

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:18.859
And Google servers can
answer you with a yes or no.

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:20.234
And the very
interesting thing is

00:17:20.234 --> 00:17:22.930
that this yes or no is a
cryptographically-sealed

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:26.700
promise from Google
because it's hardware bound

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:28.420
and it's leverage uses the TPM.

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:30.880
And it's very different from
everything else on the market

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:33.720
where these checks are much
more based on heuristics.

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:35.410
So if you take the
same kind of software

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.600
that does integrity
checks on other platforms,

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:39.100
it will check, for
example, is there

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:42.400
a lock screen, is
there an anti-virus

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:43.900
running on the device, right?

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:47.680
So you get these checks they
are actually hardware bound

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:51.040
and this is very, very
unique in the laptop market.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:53.290
And again, it's a combination
of delivering things

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:55.039
from the Cloud, you
enroll with the Cloud,

00:17:55.039 --> 00:17:58.720
but then your device boots
up and does verified boot.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:01.120
And then you can combine
the two to deliver

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:03.040
a very, very secure
implementation, which

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:08.420
is along the lines of everything
I've mentioned on the slide.

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.650
So now that you have a very
good sense of how we leverage

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:14.260
the Cloud to actually do all
these things on a Chrome device

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:15.820
and all the benefits
from that, let's

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:17.800
talk a little bit
about how we actually

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:19.600
think about Chrome
devices and where

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:21.016
we think the sweet spot is.

00:18:21.016 --> 00:18:22.390
And for us, the
sweet spot really

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:26.780
is on this intersection of
mobility and productivity.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:30.440
And as we look at the market, we
see devices like, for example,

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:36.130
the Windows Surface
or the iPad Pro,

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:38.050
and you look at them--
with the Surface is

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:40.675
a great device for productivity
because it runs the Windows OS,

00:18:40.675 --> 00:18:42.684
it has the range
of Windows apps,

00:18:42.684 --> 00:18:45.100
but then again, if you flip
it around it becomes a tablet.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.170
It's not really, doesn't really
have that mobile sense to it,

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:48.670
right?

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.350
The apps are not
optimized for touch,

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:53.470
they're not really working
well in tablet mode.

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:55.330
The iPad is kind of
the complete opposite

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.246
because it has all these
apps that work really

00:18:57.246 --> 00:19:00.350
well in the mobile sense.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.437
But then again, it doesn't
have multi-windows,

00:19:02.437 --> 00:19:04.270
it doesn't have the
things that actually you

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:06.400
would need for a
productivity-based device.

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:07.816
And what I'm going
to try and show

00:19:07.816 --> 00:19:10.250
you right now is where we
combine both with Chrome device

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:13.640
so that it really becomes a
strong contender in that space.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:15.190
So when you talk
about mobility--

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:16.570
and this was the
big announcement

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:21.980
we made almost a year now
ago in June in IO last year,

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:24.975
which was support for
Google Play on Chrome OS.

00:19:24.975 --> 00:19:26.350
And this was very,
very important

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:29.950
because it solved some very
basic problems with the Chrome

00:19:29.950 --> 00:19:31.960
devices, right?

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:33.850
Number one, it was
the app gap, right?

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:36.310
So Chrome was a
beautiful device.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:38.950
We all loved it and it had all
the things we mentioned before,

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:41.440
but then you didn't
have the breadth

00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:43.864
and the depth of apps of
the [INAUDIBLE] system

00:19:43.864 --> 00:19:44.530
that you needed.

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:45.946
And you didn't
have the developers

00:19:45.946 --> 00:19:49.390
engaged in providing
and bringing their apps

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:50.737
onto the Chrome platform.

00:19:50.737 --> 00:19:52.570
And Google Play solves
a lot of that, right?

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:55.490
Because we have a very good
story of compatibility.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:57.750
So any app that actually
runs on your Android phone

00:19:57.750 --> 00:19:59.500
is capable of running
on the Chrome device

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:01.060
without any modifications.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:03.010
So now, we actually
get all these apps

00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:06.760
that were built for Google
Play onto the Chrome device.

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:08.260
And this is very
important for us

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:11.440
because every single software
vendor, enterprise or consumer

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.810
vendor, is actually on
the Android platform.

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:16.720
And this allows us to bring
these all this capability

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:19.900
into Chrome OS, which makes it
a much, much, much better device

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:22.720
fit for, not just for mobility,
but overall enterprise

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:25.210
and consumer viability.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.970
The second one
was offline, being

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:30.190
a device that's relied
very heavily on the browser

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:33.550
and on the web meant you always
needed an internet connection.

00:20:33.550 --> 00:20:35.170
Now granted, the web
is making strides

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:37.240
towards providing offline
capability with things

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:39.600
like service workers and,
even if you look at G Suite

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:41.920
it has very, very good
offline capability,

00:20:41.920 --> 00:20:44.080
but those tend to
be the exception.

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.810
Most apps on the web are
written with online in mind

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:48.610
and some of them
support offline.

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:50.410
And, on the Play
Store you actually

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:51.707
have the complete opposite.

00:20:51.707 --> 00:20:54.040
You have apps that were written
with a very strong sense

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:58.090
of offline because most of the
time your phone is actually not

00:20:58.090 --> 00:20:59.990
connected to the internet.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:03.700
So now, we have
this huge breadth

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:07.210
of apps that actually work well
really, really well offline.

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:10.630
And once they connect
back online of course,

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:12.310
they sink their data
back to the Cloud.

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:15.470
So again, using things like
Android backup of the story,

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:18.250
even the apps, within
the logic themselves,

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:19.701
you have data backup, right?

00:21:19.701 --> 00:21:22.075
You have the offline capability
but without the downsides

00:21:22.075 --> 00:21:24.016
of offline, which
is what happens

00:21:24.016 --> 00:21:25.390
when the device
gets lost or what

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:28.180
happens when it gets stolen.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:30.040
The third one is
native capability

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:33.730
and this was something
we found lacking

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:35.260
in Chrome OS for a long time.

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:39.130
We didn't have a very
rich set of APIs.

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:42.460
So if you were a developer, say
if you were Citrix or VM or one

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:45.850
of these developers, where
you need a very elaborate API

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:48.070
surface to actually
provide a rich experience,

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:49.800
you didn't have
that on Chrome OS.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.420
But if you look
at Play developers

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.420
and what they have, in terms
of developing Android apps,

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:57.319
they have a very,
very rich set of APIs.

00:21:57.319 --> 00:21:59.110
And they can use that
now to actually bring

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:01.251
these experiences
to Chrome devices.

00:22:01.251 --> 00:22:02.750
And this brings me
to my last point,

00:22:02.750 --> 00:22:04.120
which was developers story.

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.820
And now, developers have
a very, very decent story

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:09.040
in terms of bringing
apps onto the platform.

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:09.540
Right?

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:11.860
Develop an Android app,
right, if it actually

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:13.540
needs a very rich
set of APIs, needs

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:16.270
to do hardware interaction, it
needs hardware acceleration,

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.910
it needs a touchscreen,
all these things, now you

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.310
have a very clear path
towards bringing your app over

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:24.569
onto the Chrome platform.

00:22:24.569 --> 00:22:26.110
And of course, this
was all software.

00:22:26.110 --> 00:22:28.450
So what we really need
here is the form factor

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:30.940
to match that, because
bringing in the Play Store,

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:34.540
we also want the form
factor that brings

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:36.130
the best out of the Play Store.

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.164
And these were
two of the devices

00:22:38.164 --> 00:22:39.580
we've launched
recently, which was

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:41.950
one with Samsung and with Acer.

00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:44.770
And these devices
actually become tablets

00:22:44.770 --> 00:22:46.120
and they have a stylus.

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:50.470
So it's actually
very, very interesting

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:52.702
overlap between everything
the Play Store gives you.

00:22:52.702 --> 00:22:54.160
So now, you can
use it as a tablet,

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:56.600
but then also flips
into a laptop--

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:59.240
so for all your
productivity needs as well.

00:22:59.240 --> 00:23:01.780
And the stylus of course brings
another one of those mobile

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:02.500
feels to it.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:04.560
And we're working with
different software vendors

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:10.050
and actually getting the
best out of this form factor.

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:12.499
So in looking forward,
we are actually

00:23:12.499 --> 00:23:14.290
working with a lot of
these app developers.

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:18.490
So, it will be companies like
Citrix, VMware, Mirosoft,

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:20.560
Adobe, and even
within Google itself,

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:25.330
we're working with a lot of our
first party apps, developers,

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.550
things like Hangouts,
G-mail, G Suite,

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:30.160
to actually bring the
best of these apps

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:31.780
onto the Chrome devices.

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:35.770
And moving into 2017,
and also into 2018,

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:38.680
expect to see a lot of
that, a lot more devices

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:40.570
like the ones I just
showed, which bring out

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:43.105
the best of the Play Store, but
also a lot more partnerships

00:23:43.105 --> 00:23:48.670
with app developers to bring
more desktop great capabilities

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:51.010
to these apps and make
them work really, really

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:53.950
well on a Chrome device.

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:56.260
So now that we've
looked of mobility,

00:23:56.260 --> 00:23:58.600
let's take a little
bit at productivity

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:00.610
as well because this is
not just a device that

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:03.880
can help you do your
mobile work or help you

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:05.645
when you're on the
run, it should also

00:24:05.645 --> 00:24:07.270
help you when you're
on your desk, when

00:24:07.270 --> 00:24:09.025
you want to connect
to a multi-monitor

00:24:09.025 --> 00:24:12.580
and actually do work for say,
two or three hours in a row.

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:16.340
So how does Chrome
OS fair there?

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:18.430
We think it actually
does pretty well.

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:21.970
It's a very highly
performant OS and there's

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:23.470
a lot of performance
you can get out

00:24:23.470 --> 00:24:25.780
of a Chrome device for
much less hardware.

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:28.120
And I think some people mistake
this sometimes or see it

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.495
from a wrong angle because
they think, oh well, you know,

00:24:30.495 --> 00:24:33.220
Chromebooks are
low-end hardware.

00:24:33.220 --> 00:24:35.350
Well, there's a
different spin to it

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:39.580
and it is the fact that to get
the same kind of performance

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:42.280
you get say, from
a $1,000 laptop,

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.950
you actually only need to
throw $600 worth of hardware

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:47.412
at the Chrome operating system.

00:24:47.412 --> 00:24:49.120
And this was because
the operating system

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:52.572
itself is very light and has
been built to be very light.

00:24:52.572 --> 00:24:54.280
And this doesn't
change, even as we bring

00:24:54.280 --> 00:24:55.657
the Android system onboard.

00:24:55.657 --> 00:24:57.490
Because if you think
about the Android apps,

00:24:57.490 --> 00:24:59.770
and apps from the Play
Store, they've actually all

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:03.160
been built to run on very
low-end hardware, phones,

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:05.077
ARM chips, very little memory.

00:25:05.077 --> 00:25:06.910
So now, you take those
apps and you put them

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:09.490
on a laptop, which
has an Intel processor

00:25:09.490 --> 00:25:12.310
and these apps are
really, really snappy.

00:25:12.310 --> 00:25:15.070
So all these things combined,
the lightness of the OS

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:17.200
and the fact that all the
apps were built anyway

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:20.050
for low-end hardware, means you
can throw much less hardware

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:21.610
to get the same experience.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.400
So now, if you want to
do a premium Chromebook,

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:27.370
we would not need to throw
more than $1,000 of hardware

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:29.140
on it to match
what you would need

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:34.420
to buy for $2000 from
a different vendor.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:38.044
And moving onto
that, we also make

00:25:38.044 --> 00:25:40.210
sure that the experience
within the operating system

00:25:40.210 --> 00:25:42.000
supports these
productive use cases.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:45.430
So, not only have we brought all
the apps from the Play Store,

00:25:45.430 --> 00:25:47.440
but we make sure the
integration of these apps

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:48.700
actually works really well.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:50.759
So we look at things
like multi-sized windows.

00:25:50.759 --> 00:25:52.300
You can actually
resize your windows,

00:25:52.300 --> 00:25:56.470
things you're actually used
to doing on a laptop device,

00:25:56.470 --> 00:25:59.835
but they weren't really part
of how Android was built--

00:25:59.835 --> 00:26:01.210
look at things
like multitasking,

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:05.320
multi-profiles,
integration with keyboard,

00:26:05.320 --> 00:26:08.470
integration with multi-monitors.

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:09.970
So all these things
we try really

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:12.340
hard to make them work
really well because we do

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:15.170
want this to be perceived
as a highly performant OS,

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:18.970
as an OS that really works
for enterprise workloads.

00:26:18.970 --> 00:26:20.560
Now, the second part
of this, and this

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:23.920
is where it really excels, is
we have desktop-grade enterprise

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:25.080
browser as well.

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:27.430
If you look at Chrome
browser across all platforms,

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:29.530
but especially on Chrome
OS, it is actually

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:32.020
a very, very powerful browser.

00:26:32.020 --> 00:26:33.687
So even now when we
have the Play Store,

00:26:33.687 --> 00:26:35.436
we actually don't bring
the Chrome browser

00:26:35.436 --> 00:26:36.280
from the Play Store.

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:39.400
We use the same Chrome browser
we have on Linux and on Mac

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:43.006
because we do want you to have
this desktop-grade experience--

00:26:43.006 --> 00:26:46.090
pushing extensions that have
to do with productivity.

00:26:46.090 --> 00:26:50.830
And it is fully manageable,
as is all of the other OS.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:53.080
So everything we
said about management

00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:55.007
applies to the Chrome
browser as well.

00:26:55.007 --> 00:26:56.590
And it's very, very
highly performant,

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:57.850
even under massive workloads.

00:26:57.850 --> 00:27:00.320
So depending how much hardware
you have in your hand,

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:02.440
you can open up 20,40, 50 tabs.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:06.850
The thing will actually be able
to run really well with them.

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:09.280
And just to make
sure we know as well

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:15.070
that, this is not just for
when you have a laptop in hand

00:27:15.070 --> 00:27:17.570
because what we are doing now,
and based on the form factors

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:20.320
we're coming up with,
which can become tablets,

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:22.420
we are making sure that
the Chrome browser, even

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:26.170
the desktop version of it, works
well and is touch-optimized

00:27:26.170 --> 00:27:28.292
and it's working
offline capable.

00:27:28.292 --> 00:27:30.250
So we're bringing the
newest versions of Chrome

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:32.500
that have technologies
like service workers,

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:35.335
but we also optimizing a UI
so that it's touch-enabled

00:27:35.335 --> 00:27:37.460
so that when you do flip
into a tablet form factor,

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:39.437
or using the touchscreen,
Chrome browser, even

00:27:39.437 --> 00:27:41.770
in it's desktop version, is
working really, really well.

00:27:44.810 --> 00:27:50.660
And one last point
here is that as we

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:52.610
drove these enterprise
integrations and we

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:59.090
brought better support for
enterprise use cases, what

00:27:59.090 --> 00:27:59.900
we've seen--

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:01.670
and this is a big
part of the work we've

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:03.260
done over the last few years--

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:05.720
is that integrations across
the board for everything

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:07.220
that enterprises
need, so everything

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:10.010
ranging from identity,
networking, certificates,

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:12.470
storage, and virtualization,
all these things

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:16.340
we're actually not fully
possible on a Chrome device

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:18.219
if you look three years ago.

00:28:18.219 --> 00:28:19.760
If you look at
identity, for example,

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:21.343
the only way to
log-in to a Chromebook

00:28:21.343 --> 00:28:22.869
was through a Google account.

00:28:22.869 --> 00:28:25.160
Now, you can actually log-in
with your Active Directory

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:28.100
account, Ping, Okta, Clever, so
whatever your identity provider

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:31.490
is, as long as it's
Salmon compliant,

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:34.850
it can actually allow you to
log-in to the Chrome device

00:28:34.850 --> 00:28:35.720
with it.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:38.180
If you look at
storage, the only way

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:40.460
to do storage, like Cloud
Storage on current devices,

00:28:40.460 --> 00:28:42.560
in the beginning was
through Google Drive.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:45.855
But now, we've added support for
third party storage providers

00:28:45.855 --> 00:28:48.440
so now, if you're a Box
or Dropbox customer,

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:52.520
or even you have your own
network fileshare on Pram,

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:55.460
you can connect the
Chrome device to that.

00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:58.250
And for all legacy app
needs, whether you're

00:28:58.250 --> 00:29:00.350
doing full desktop
virtualization or just app

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:02.510
virtualization-- working
very, very closely

00:29:02.510 --> 00:29:04.880
with vendors like
Citrix, VM, or Amazon

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:07.770
WorkSpaces-- to bring the best
of virtualization to the Chrome

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:10.970
device as well so that
even as you migrate away

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:13.970
from the Windows
environment into the web

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:17.240
and into Android workloads,
you can still get access

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:22.790
to the old apps that you still
need to run for your users.

00:29:22.790 --> 00:29:25.580
And just to also mention, that
we mentioned form factors are

00:29:25.580 --> 00:29:27.380
convertibles as well,
but that doesn't mean

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:28.963
we're not actually
working really hard

00:29:28.963 --> 00:29:31.250
to also produce
really great laptops.

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:33.890
And this is where I put this in
more of the productivity sense.

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:35.390
These do have, many
of these laptops

00:29:35.390 --> 00:29:37.430
actually have touch
screens, but they

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:39.650
are made to be more like
fully-formed laptops.

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:41.360
And we are working
with these vendors,

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:43.970
with OEMs like HP,
like Lenovo, to bring,

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:46.190
for example-- this is Lenovo's
ThinkPad-- to actually

00:29:46.190 --> 00:29:48.590
bring those devices
onto the market

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:51.560
because we do believe that there
is a very big market for this.

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:55.160
And we want to have more
and more high-end specs.

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:57.080
Because for EDU we
had a lot of success

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.520
in the lower end of
the hardware spectrum,

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:02.840
but we realized enterprises
have much, much bigger needs

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:03.939
hardware-wise.

00:30:03.939 --> 00:30:05.480
So we are working
with these vendors,

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:08.600
actually bring all
that onto the platform.

00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:11.120
So with this, I'm going
to hand over back to Seb

00:30:11.120 --> 00:30:14.090
because, to give
you a break from me,

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:15.860
but also because he
wants to tell you

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:18.560
about how all these things,
we're actually seeing them

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:23.450
in the field and how customers
are using all those value

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:24.950
propositions from
the Chrome devices

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:27.770
to make very, very
large deployments.

00:30:27.770 --> 00:30:28.320
Thank you.

00:30:31.104 --> 00:30:33.580
SEBASTIAN SCHEITER: So I
really don't have too much more

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:35.870
to add to David's
points, but there

00:30:35.870 --> 00:30:38.600
are a couple of things I
do want to highlight when

00:30:38.600 --> 00:30:41.630
we look at this and
how this actually

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:43.462
translates into the field.

00:30:43.462 --> 00:30:45.170
And I'll hopefully
share some sound bites

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:47.270
from customers like
yourself who are actually

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:52.020
using this beyond the
Chromebook form factor.

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:56.470
I want to pivot the narrative
just a little bit away

00:30:56.470 --> 00:31:01.270
from Chromebook or any
specific Chrome OS form factor

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:04.450
and really want to focus
on Chrome as the underlying

00:31:04.450 --> 00:31:07.720
system for virtually
any of your screens

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:11.140
in the enterprise, whether it be
the knowledge worker, the power

00:31:11.140 --> 00:31:16.030
user, whether it be
for signs, and thus

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:19.390
contextual information, whether
it's basically Chrome powering

00:31:19.390 --> 00:31:27.220
a kiosk, or for that matter,
video conferencing technology.

00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:28.980
The reality is that
one common theme

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:33.330
that we hear from CIOs in
the field in IT leadership

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:38.480
is that they consider Chrome
as a future-proof platform that

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:41.912
allows them to deliver on their
two to three year roadmap.

00:31:41.912 --> 00:31:43.370
So I just want to
pause there and I

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:44.536
want to just underline that.

00:31:44.536 --> 00:31:46.310
That's really, really important.

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:48.860
So we're moving away from
this narrative of Chrome

00:31:48.860 --> 00:31:54.200
being a hardware player
and we're really--

00:31:54.200 --> 00:31:55.940
the message that
is resonating is

00:31:55.940 --> 00:31:57.830
that Chrome is now a
full-fledged operating

00:31:57.830 --> 00:32:00.800
system that can virtually
power all your screens, right?

00:32:00.800 --> 00:32:02.900
So when we talk
to IT leadership,

00:32:02.900 --> 00:32:06.380
rather than looking at this
as a very tactical hardware

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:09.380
replacement game, this is
becoming much, much, much

00:32:09.380 --> 00:32:11.710
more strategic.

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:14.080
Now, in reality,
and we know this

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:16.300
because we've toured
a lot of companies,

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:20.030
every CIO has a mandate
to move into the Cloud,

00:32:20.030 --> 00:32:22.060
whether it's
infrastructure, whether it's

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:25.280
a platform as a service, whether
it's software as a service,

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:28.240
whether it's fully
embracing GCP,

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:31.660
or whether it's to deploy
that one single app that

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:33.940
lives in the Cloud
that has to be deployed

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:39.070
to numerous endpoints that
are geographically dispersed.

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:41.740
We think, and this
is something, again,

00:32:41.740 --> 00:32:43.630
that echoes in the
field, that Chrome

00:32:43.630 --> 00:32:47.320
can play a really, really
important role to make

00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:48.950
that smooth transition
into the Cloud.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:56.360
And while we, frankly,
think about future-proofing,

00:32:56.360 --> 00:33:02.490
we really want to make sure
that the customer and ourselves,

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:06.540
we really put these four
core pillars back into focus.

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:11.050
Because at the end of the day,
and I know this is repetitive,

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:14.140
these are the key attributes
that virtually any success

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:16.300
or any project that we're
going to be involved with

00:33:16.300 --> 00:33:19.360
is going to be measured against,
speed, simplicity, security,

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:20.840
shareability.

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:24.400
And personally, I think we
can truly help with these four

00:33:24.400 --> 00:33:26.350
attributes.

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:29.740
Anecdotally speed-- I was
told not to use the logo

00:33:29.740 --> 00:33:30.870
and not to use the name.

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:33.270
So I'll try to be as cryptic
as possible here, but--

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:36.160
speed, we have a customer
in the Chicago area

00:33:36.160 --> 00:33:41.860
that is essentially using Chrome
kiosks attached to a touch

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:45.580
screen where associates
on the retail floor

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:46.990
input customer data.

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:51.190
That data then gets sent to
the cashier and at that point,

00:33:51.190 --> 00:33:53.960
hopefully, the sale
gets converted.

00:33:53.960 --> 00:33:56.200
Right?

00:33:56.200 --> 00:34:00.040
They came to us because of speed
because even a 10 second lag

00:34:00.040 --> 00:34:04.970
in that transaction actually
impacts the bottom line.

00:34:04.970 --> 00:34:08.929
An unscheduled and unplanned
reboot of that terminal

00:34:08.929 --> 00:34:13.030
has detrimental
effects to their PNL.

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:16.730
When we look at
simplicity and we

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:19.250
preach the story
of, we're Chrome

00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:22.852
and we want to power multiple
endpoints, multiple screens,

00:34:22.852 --> 00:34:24.560
we've got to make sure
that the user that

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:27.110
is using each and every
one of those screens

00:34:27.110 --> 00:34:32.810
has the same user experience
and has the same user interface

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:35.620
across all these screens.

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:40.550
Security, this is arguably
one of, I would say,

00:34:40.550 --> 00:34:43.639
the loudest USPs that
we hear in the field

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:45.200
where people want
to talk to us, I

00:34:45.200 --> 00:34:47.150
have a really cool story there.

00:34:47.150 --> 00:34:50.179
We have a customer that
actually has a corporate policy

00:34:50.179 --> 00:34:52.280
that when their
executives travel abroad

00:34:52.280 --> 00:34:54.739
they're supposed to hand
in their primary device

00:34:54.739 --> 00:35:00.920
and pick up a Chromebook for
the duration of their trip.

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:02.960
In that specific
case, we actually

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:06.050
boot into a virtual desktop.

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:08.960
God forbid, if that device
then gets lost or left

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:12.320
in a cab in Shanghai
or Berlin, it's a $200

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:15.330
write-off versus a pretty
substantial data leak.

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:23.630
And then shareability, if you
listen to what David said,

00:35:23.630 --> 00:35:30.110
you can sense that this paradigm
of one user to one device

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:31.910
really no longer exists.

00:35:31.910 --> 00:35:33.770
We ran some numbers
with our customers

00:35:33.770 --> 00:35:36.530
and it turns out that
over the course of a day

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:40.460
the power worker, the power
user, uses five devices.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:44.070
So we're starting to
see this 5 to 1 ratio.

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:47.360
And arguably, if that's the
case, that we need to make sure

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:50.330
that you take a
device, you log in

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:53.810
or you not log in via
certain kiosk sessions,

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:55.310
and it becomes a
seamless experience

00:35:55.310 --> 00:35:57.726
when, at the end of the day
you pick up a different device

00:35:57.726 --> 00:36:01.340
or you go home and continue
where you left off three

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:03.440
to four hours before that.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:05.207
and that's really,
really important.

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:14.060
Now, if you take the device
management and layer them

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:19.340
on top of these four
core principles,

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:23.330
this becomes a very, very,
very powerful enterprise story.

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:25.261
Because yes, Chrome--
we all get this

00:36:25.261 --> 00:36:27.260
and we all believe in
this-- but all of a sudden

00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:31.550
you can take devices and
you can repurpose them

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:33.530
and you can allow
the shareability

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:37.510
by a centralized Cloud-based
Management Console.

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:42.110
We have customers that have
thousands of devices deployed

00:36:42.110 --> 00:36:46.130
in the field that are catering
to 100 different use cases all

00:36:46.130 --> 00:36:51.200
based on policies that one
IT admin in North America

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:53.680
is essentially administering.

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:56.180
And David has said this before,
whether it's a user setting,

00:36:56.180 --> 00:36:58.054
whether it's a hardware
setting, whether it's

00:36:58.054 --> 00:37:02.820
a certificate setting, all this
can essentially be manipulated,

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:10.310
pardon the strong word,
down to the device level

00:37:10.310 --> 00:37:14.000
So let me put a little bit
of meat to the bone here.

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:18.960
Specifically in retail and
specifically in manufacturing,

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:21.410
we have seen Chrome
devices being used

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:24.820
to optimize employee workflows.

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:27.120
And one of my colleagues
on the strategy side and I,

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:30.610
we went and
interviewed customers

00:37:30.610 --> 00:37:33.820
and we basically asked, what
is the problem statement

00:37:33.820 --> 00:37:36.010
that we should be solving for?

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:38.970
And this is
essentially the output.

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:45.100
This idea that, , throughout the
course of the day you have one

00:37:45.100 --> 00:37:50.690
device that caters to multiple
users, to multiple use cases.

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:53.330
Historically, if you look
at all these segments,

00:37:53.330 --> 00:37:57.650
those would have been a fleet
of devices, but in this case,

00:37:57.650 --> 00:38:01.000
the Chromebook starts in
the back of the office,

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:05.405
the user logs in based on
his or her user settings,

00:38:05.405 --> 00:38:07.030
but throughout the
day, and again, this

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:09.970
is someone retail biased,
throughout the day that device

00:38:09.970 --> 00:38:14.480
actually migrates to
the front of the store.

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:19.680
That device then becomes a
product look-up, a catalog.

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.380
It becomes a digital sign.

00:38:21.380 --> 00:38:24.080
In some cases even, it becomes
something that can give you

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:27.900
contextual information.

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:29.790
In this very specific
example actually,

00:38:29.790 --> 00:38:32.380
this device at some point gets
handed over to the customer

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:36.900
and it floats around the actual
brick and mortar real estate.

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:40.200
And at the end of the day, the
employee picks up the device

00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:42.720
and puts it back
on the Chrome cart.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:45.150
That's a pretty cool story
given that this is also

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:46.857
a $300 endpoint.

00:38:46.857 --> 00:38:48.690
Historically, you would
have solved for that

00:38:48.690 --> 00:38:53.450
with multiple $1,000 endpoints.

00:38:53.450 --> 00:38:57.680
So I always like to call it the
conversion-on-the-fly scenario,

00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:00.290
but if you think about it, this
is now really a departure from

00:39:00.290 --> 00:39:02.630
the Chromebook and EDU story.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:06.590
This is something where we're
actually impacting workflows

00:39:06.590 --> 00:39:09.230
and we're trying to
create effectiveness

00:39:09.230 --> 00:39:13.290
amongst the associates.

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:16.004
One really additional
example here, specifically

00:39:16.004 --> 00:39:17.670
in the manufacturing
space and if you've

00:39:17.670 --> 00:39:21.180
attended some of the previous
sessions you know this,

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:22.860
is what our good
friends at Sanmina

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:24.460
are doing actually
down the road.

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:28.110
Sanmina, large
manufacturing company,

00:39:28.110 --> 00:39:32.010
part of the Fortune
500, they basically,

00:39:32.010 --> 00:39:37.890
are using Chrome OS devices to
replace paper-based processes.

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:41.780
And they started on their
manufacturing floor creating,

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:45.440
and this looks somewhat
manual, but I can guarantee you

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:47.150
it's actually
pretty cool, they've

00:39:47.150 --> 00:39:49.370
taken a large
touchscreen, they have

00:39:49.370 --> 00:39:51.380
taken a Chrome box,
attached it to it,

00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:54.740
and they're booting up multiple
kiosks in multiple apps

00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:58.040
based on the user, based
on that user's credential.

00:39:58.040 --> 00:40:01.280
For temp workers, those
people don't actually

00:40:01.280 --> 00:40:03.080
have to log into
the application.

00:40:03.080 --> 00:40:05.390
It just boots up
a public session,

00:40:05.390 --> 00:40:06.800
what I would
consider, basically,

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:09.480
a managed guest mode.

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:15.040
Now, they have increased
these employees efficiencies,

00:40:15.040 --> 00:40:18.070
but what's nice is they
came to us and said,

00:40:18.070 --> 00:40:21.760
we believe in Chrome being
the underlying platform

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:24.310
for our future and we
need to start somewhere.

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:26.800
And this is essentially
where they started.

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:30.790
So they created the subset of
users, they deployed rapidly,

00:40:30.790 --> 00:40:33.580
they had about 2000 of these
endpoints in the field right

00:40:33.580 --> 00:40:39.560
now, and are now organically
growing into other use cases.

00:40:39.560 --> 00:40:43.107
And as Rajin, our lead
PM, said earlier today,

00:40:43.107 --> 00:40:45.190
they're now really focused
on the knowledge worker

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:46.780
and making that happen.

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:50.230
One caveat, I should say is,
they are G Suite customer

00:40:50.230 --> 00:40:52.240
and I believe they're
a GCP customer as well.

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:55.300
So they are going in
all Google, but it's

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:58.780
great to see that obviously
devices and Chrome are

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:00.240
one of those
substantial endpoints

00:41:00.240 --> 00:41:06.430
and workflows to make this
one Google story happen.

00:41:06.430 --> 00:41:10.300
Now, one other
great example where

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:13.570
a company has started small
and the story and the adoption

00:41:13.570 --> 00:41:16.480
has really grown rapidly
and grown organically,

00:41:16.480 --> 00:41:18.700
is what our friends here at
Charles Schwab are doing.

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:21.880
And may the demo
gods be with me,

00:41:21.880 --> 00:41:25.710
I'm going to try to play
this video for everyone.

00:41:25.710 --> 00:41:29.220
And he's much more articulate
than I am so, here we go.

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:32.850
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:41:32.850 --> 00:41:35.020
- There are multiple sides
of Schwab's business.

00:41:35.020 --> 00:41:37.200
And one of those sides
is the advisor side

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:38.430
or the institutional side.

00:41:38.430 --> 00:41:41.460
And obviously, we offer a lot of
products and a lot of services

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:43.030
to our institutional customers.

00:41:43.030 --> 00:41:45.780
I'm responsible for making
sure that we deliver on behalf

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:46.870
of what our clients need.

00:41:46.870 --> 00:41:49.110
As we were looking for
solutions, bringing about

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:51.390
a device to the
branches where again,

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:53.400
clients could very easily
open up an account,

00:41:53.400 --> 00:41:55.590
we reached out to our
colleagues over at Google

00:41:55.590 --> 00:41:58.260
and we asked them whether or
not Google's Chromebook was

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:00.300
something that would
be applicable to what

00:42:00.300 --> 00:42:01.380
we were trying to solve.

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:02.310
That was on a Friday.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:06.690
On Monday morning we had two
of their top engineers engaged

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:08.640
with my engineers here.

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:11.029
3 and 1/2 hours later,
I get a knock on my door

00:42:11.029 --> 00:42:12.820
and I'll never forget
what they said to me,

00:42:12.820 --> 00:42:14.470
they said, we've solved it.

00:42:14.470 --> 00:42:17.820
Google offered a number of
services and capabilities

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:19.470
within their Chromebook
platform that

00:42:19.470 --> 00:42:20.950
were incredibly attractive.

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:25.000
One of them was the ability to
remotely control the devices.

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:26.890
We could force them
onto a specific network,

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:28.922
our network, if they
fell off that network,

00:42:28.922 --> 00:42:29.880
they would be unusable.

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:32.505
If someone stole them we had the
ability to remotely wipe them.

00:42:32.505 --> 00:42:34.838
I would say one of the unique
features of the Chromebook

00:42:34.838 --> 00:42:36.430
has to do with their
operating system.

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:38.430
The device isn't burdened
with a lot of legacy

00:42:38.430 --> 00:42:39.740
or with a large OS.

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:42.540
Instead, the experience
is, you open the Chromebook

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:44.190
and 10 seconds
later you're online.

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:46.560
Because of the kiosk capability
that the Chromebook had

00:42:46.560 --> 00:42:48.420
to offer, when
one of our clients

00:42:48.420 --> 00:42:50.350
came in to open
up a SIP account,

00:42:50.350 --> 00:42:51.600
it was start up a new session.

00:42:51.600 --> 00:42:54.540
And as soon as I Chromebook
was closed, session gone,

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:55.530
everything gets erased.

00:42:55.530 --> 00:42:57.120
The full roll out
and development

00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:00.220
started with a number of
weeks of user engagement.

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:02.675
We would take the Chromebooks
out to specific branches,

00:43:02.675 --> 00:43:04.050
test it out, and
then from there,

00:43:04.050 --> 00:43:05.970
it was making sure
that the device

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:08.260
could stand up and do exactly
what we need it to do,

00:43:08.260 --> 00:43:09.200
but do it at scale.

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:11.310
When you're working with a firm
that's got hundreds of branches

00:43:11.310 --> 00:43:13.476
out there and you're going
to eventually deploy well

00:43:13.476 --> 00:43:15.510
over 1,000 of these
devices, the ability

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:17.910
to control them from
a central location

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:19.050
is absolutely paramount.

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:21.910
Really, the Chromebook was
the one that met our needs.

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:24.060
They work at any
time, all the time.

00:43:24.060 --> 00:43:29.889
[END PLAYBACK]

00:43:29.889 --> 00:43:31.680
SEBASTIAN SCHEITER: If
some of you noticed,

00:43:31.680 --> 00:43:33.900
that was still our old
logo, Google for Work,

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:37.900
but no one saw that.

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:43.110
So I was very closely
involved with the Schwab deal

00:43:43.110 --> 00:43:44.280
and the Schwab use case.

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:47.100
And again, a great
example where they

00:43:47.100 --> 00:43:49.560
had a very clearly
defined problem

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:54.610
statement, a very limited time
to get something converted.

00:43:54.610 --> 00:43:56.550
And we got to work.

00:43:56.550 --> 00:43:58.920
The reason I would
say it worked so well

00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:03.030
is because, quite frankly, the
only ask that I would always

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:06.960
have engaging with clients is
giving the problem statement

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:09.600
and predefine the users
that we should work with

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:11.167
and we will solve it out.

00:44:11.167 --> 00:44:12.750
And if it's not a
fit, it's not a fit,

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:13.958
but that's how I would start.

00:44:16.440 --> 00:44:18.480
These are not my
words, but they're

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.100
from the Wall Street Journal--

00:44:20.100 --> 00:44:23.400
this was this happened
late last year where,

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:25.560
again, after a pretty
strong showing last year

00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:29.880
we really saw traction
and saw momentum,

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:32.580
saw credible momentum in
the enterprise and adoption

00:44:32.580 --> 00:44:35.040
across a bunch of key verticals.

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:39.580
And so, I would
encourage you to try

00:44:39.580 --> 00:44:43.030
to take that seriously
and look at Chrome really

00:44:43.030 --> 00:44:46.420
as a formidable
player in that space.

00:44:46.420 --> 00:44:50.820
When prospects and
customers ask me,

00:44:50.820 --> 00:44:54.100
that's great, Seb, but
where do we go from here?

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:55.950
How do we even
tackle this thing?

00:44:55.950 --> 00:44:59.490
Because we all know, either
from a client perspective

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:01.260
or even from a
manufacturing perspective,

00:45:01.260 --> 00:45:05.430
tackling the OS space is 35
years of legacy architecture.

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:06.930
That's really, really scary.

00:45:06.930 --> 00:45:14.180
So the piece of advice I would
have is, start small, deploy

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:18.090
quickly, and grow organically.

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:21.360
And what do I mean by that?

00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:25.250
If you start small,
again, pre-defined users,

00:45:25.250 --> 00:45:29.340
give us a problem statement
that we can solve.

00:45:29.340 --> 00:45:30.840
So in the Sanmina
use case it was

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:33.570
basically replacing
paper-based processes,

00:45:33.570 --> 00:45:37.560
doing something very, very
tangible within their supply

00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:39.120
chain, within their workflow.

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:40.140
Deploy quickly.

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:43.410
I know we live in this
world of pilots and POCs,

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:45.330
and they're all real
and we shouldn't

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:49.680
forget about them, but in
reality, give the device

00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:53.170
or give the solution to the
user and let them decide.

00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:54.720
I'm a big fan of
just, basically,

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:56.820
ripping or replacing and
just seeing if it works

00:45:56.820 --> 00:45:58.945
and if it doesn't work then
that's a valid feedback

00:45:58.945 --> 00:46:00.130
and we'll move on.

00:46:00.130 --> 00:46:03.282
So the deployment time
frame is something really,

00:46:03.282 --> 00:46:03.990
really important.

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:07.570
Give it to the user, let
them decide whether it works.

00:46:07.570 --> 00:46:09.390
And if we do our
job right, then I

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:12.990
do think that there
is this organic growth

00:46:12.990 --> 00:46:17.460
pattern within the enterprise
across all your screens

00:46:17.460 --> 00:46:19.320
that will make
Chrome successful.

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:22.045
And that's really, frankly,
what we're here to do,

00:46:22.045 --> 00:46:23.670
and that's what my
team is there to do,

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:25.500
and that's what David's
team is here to do.

00:46:25.500 --> 00:46:28.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

